// Regression test: assigning a value to a struct field must be type-checked
// against that field's declared type, same as assigning to an ordinary
// variable. Originally written against the old brace-literal struct
// constructor (`User { age: "not a number", name: "Alice" }`, since removed
// -- see StructName::new(...)/StructName::default()) -- before the fix,
// TypeChecker::visitStructInit() only checked for UNKNOWN field names (a
// typo'd field), never that a correctly-named field's VALUE actually
// matched its declared type, and `age: "not a number"` (age declared as
// int) compiled with ZERO diagnostics, storing the string literal's raw
// pointer bit-pattern directly into the field's i64 storage slot: silent
// type confusion producing garbage data (confirmed: printing the resulting
// `age` gave a nonsense value like 102697087172654, not even a crash to
// signal something was wrong). Re-expressed here as a direct field
// assignment (`u.age = ...`), the modern equivalent of supplying a field
// value -- still exercises ordinary field-typed assignment checking, the
// same underlying guarantee this test exists to protect.

struct User {
    age: int,
    name: string
}

fn main(): int {
    mut u: User = User::default();
    u.age = "not a number";
    return 0;
}
